{:scenarios
 ;; reset.sh STOPS AT THE MULLIGAN PHASE (#320). It did the keep-hand steps
 ;; itself until 7480fbc11 (2026-01-30), which removed them so a human could
 ;; read card text before deciding; this file was never updated and kept
 ;; describing a precondition it no longer established, which is why it reads
 ;; as fine. Every scenario below must answer both mulligans first, or the
 ;; first action is refused and nothing in the game ever moves.
 {:fresh-game
  {:desc "Game start, both players have kept their opening hands"
   :setup [{:corp "keep-hand"}
           {:runner "keep-hand"}]}

  ;; NOT USABLE YET, and now says so. Measured 2026-11-11, driving each step:
  ;; the two keep-hands and `start-turn` all succeed, but `corp end-turn --force`
  ;; then exits 0 while leaving a hand-size prompt open ("Discard down to 5
  ;; cards"), and the Runner still reads {:can-start false :reason
  ;; :opponent-not-ended}. So the Corp's turn does NOT end and the Runner's
  ;; never begins. The step is left OUT rather than included as a lie, and the
  ;; :desc below describes what the setup actually establishes.
  ;;
  ;; Reaching the Runner's turn needs the Corp to resolve that discard first,
  ;; i.e. prompt handling — which is exactly what the commented-out tests at the
  ;; bottom of this file already say they are waiting on. Whoever adds it should
  ;; drive the steps, not reason about them: this scenario's previous :desc
  ;; claimed a state it could not reach, which is the #320 defect over again.
  ;;
  ;; RENAMED from :runner-turn-start (review finding). A comment explaining that
  ;; the scenario does not reach the Runner's turn does not help a test author
  ;; who read the KEY and believed it; the two commented-out tests below are
  ;; Runner actions that would have been refused :opponent-has-clicks on a
  ;; fixture whose name promised otherwise. The key now says what it establishes.
  :corp-turn-started
  {:desc "Corp's turn started, hands kept — the Runner's turn has NOT begun yet"
   :setup [{:corp "keep-hand"}
           {:runner "keep-hand"}
           {:corp "start-turn"}]}}

 :tests
 [{:name "basic-command-execution"
   :desc "Validates that basic commands execute without error"
   :scenario :fresh-game
   :actions [{:corp "take-credit"}
             {:corp "take-credit"}
             {:corp "take-credit"}]
   :expect-log-contains
   ["gain 1"]
   ;; THREE clicks, so three lines. `expect-log-contains` alone is satisfied by
   ;; one of the three landing (#320). The count is 3 and not "at least one"
   ;; because status prints the last FOUR log entries, which here are the
   ;; mandatory start-of-turn draw plus exactly these three — measured, not
   ;; assumed. If a future engine line lands inside that window this count is
   ;; what will say so.
   :expect-log-count
   {:pattern "gain 1" :count 3}
   :timeout 10000}

